# Paths excluded from Cycode SAST as false positives. Keep entries file-scoped.

# llk-audit: a local-only developer audit tool, never built or shipped. Cycode
# reports "unsanitized dynamic input in file path" on the os.path.join/open calls
# built from argparse arguments, and "unsanitized user input in OS command" on a
# subprocess.run that is passed a list with shell=False. Every path comes from an
# argument an engineer typed or a build log they produced; the file opens are
# read-only and already guarded by try/except OSError. The rule's remediation
# cannot apply to capture.py: confining paths under the repo root would break it,
# because JIT kernel build dirs live at $HOME/.cache/tt-metal-cache and are its input.
tt_metal/tt-llk/.claude/tools/llk-audit/kernel_tier/capture.py
tt_metal/tt-llk/.claude/tools/llk-audit/llkaudit/cli.py
tt_metal/tt-llk/.claude/tools/llk-audit/llkaudit/registry.py

# streaming-profiler tests: Cycode reports "unsanitized user input in OS command" on the subprocess.run
# calls that shell out to tracy-csvexport and to the workload binary. Each is passed a list with
# shell=False, so no shell parses the arguments and no injection is reachable; the tool paths are module
# constants and the capture path is created by the test itself. The rule's remediation -- hardcode every
# input -- cannot apply to a tool under the build tree or a capture named per run.
# NOTE: this file is honoured by the Cycode CLI only -- the PR bot scans the diff server-side and does not
# read it, so an entry here never suppresses a review comment.
tests/ttnn/tracy/test_streaming_profiler.py

# teardown sanity + the tt-run launcher: Cycode reports "unsanitized user input in OS command" on the subprocess
# Popen calls that start the MPI launcher (tt-run / mpirun). Each is passed an argument LIST with shell=False, so no
# shell parses the arguments and no injection is reachable; the argv is the launcher line tt-run builds from its own
# rank binding and --mpi-args, not external input. The rule's remediation -- hardcode the command -- cannot apply to
# a launcher whose command is the job it was asked to run. (The PR bot scans the diff server-side and does not read
# this file, so the review comment is resolved separately.)
ttnn/ttnn/distributed/ttrun.py
tests/tt_metal/distributed/multiprocess/teardown_sanity/sanity_common.py
